<!-- CAN BODY, SEAM, AND STRUCTURAL INTEGRITY ISSUES -->
<section class="section" id="can-body-seam-and-structural-integrity-issues">
  <h2>Can Body, Seam, and Structural Integrity Issues</h2>
  <p>While less frequent than valve leaks, failures in the can body or seams can occur due to internal pressure stress combined with material weaknesses. Propellant pressure continuously loads the side seam, bottom dome, and internal coating. If any of these elements are compromised, leakage may develop gradually.</p>

    <p>Internal corrosion, coating defects, or metal thinning can weaken structural zones without immediate visible signs. In such cases, leakage may appear as pressure loss rather than liquid escape, making detection more difficult.</p>

    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="list-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Structural leakage contributors</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Side seam integrity loss under pressure</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Bottom dome deformation</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Internal coating degradation</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Stress concentration at formed metal zones</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    
